    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "db604732-c897-57e7-a895-6c2b13ee9528",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186129154/plots/c0670d7ae62816c1cca2482b6e8cbcc91524ef64037cc8c47b5567d3959aafd0.png)\n\nThe graph of a polynomial $p(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x=-1$ is a root of even multiplicity.\n\n- **II.** $x=1$ is **not** a simple root.\n\n- **III.** $x=1$ is a root of even multiplicity.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The root $x=-1$ has an odd multiplicity because at $x=-1$ the graph passes through the $x$-axis at this point.\n\n- Statement II is true. The root $x=1$ is not a simple root because the graph crosses the $x$-axis at that point, and the curve is tangent to the $x$-axis at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. The root $x=1$ has an odd multiplicity because at $x=1$ the graph passes through the $x$-axis at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\".",
