      ":question/id": "267dd9b5-d1b8-5733-8091-b33d66efae4c",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186129154/plots/deff5c31720c011dee4a00dc90e3de4327316270c11517fea4d9eea6baaf56df.png)\n\nThe graph of a polynomial $p(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x=1$ is a simple root.\n- **II.** $x=4$ is a root of odd multiplicity.\n- **III.** $x=4$ is a simple root.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The root $x=1$ is a simple root because the graph crosses the $x$-axis at the point and the curve is not tangent to the $x$-axis at that point.\n\n- Statement II is true. The root $x=4$ has an odd multiplicity because at $x=4$ the graph passes through the $x$-axis at this point.\n\n- Statement III is false. The root $x=4$ is not a simple root because the graph crosses the $x$-axis at that point, and the curve is tangent to the $x$-axis at that point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\".",
