    "question": {
      ":question/id": "9cf56545-726c-5164-9a1f-86f7d4f5460b",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186129154/plots/bc720effab09ecca52be2051495db8860fbdabbbc61754bff5fc821cbad46d7c.png)\n\nThe graph of a polynomial $p(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x=5$ is a simple root.\n\n- **II.** $x=2$ is a root of even multiplicity.\n\n- **III.** $p(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The root $x=5$ is a simple root because the graph crosses the $x$-axis at the point and the curve is not tangent to the $x$-axis at that point.\n\n- Statement II is true. The root $x=2$  has an even multiplicity because at $x=2$ the graph just touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction.\n\n- Statement III is false. As $x$ moves right in the positive direction towards $+\\infty ,$ the $y$ value moves up in the positive direction towards $+\\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\".",
